Refurbished Linux 0.3.0-r6 — hardware: Wi-Fi password, polkit, brightness, volume

file:   refurbished-linux-0.3.0-amd64.iso
size:   824180736 bytes
sha256: 5cdce8ee0e9dfba8d4f1ec6324887504c3d50822fb9318605bdf481c9bbe2e7e

Root causes found in the running system:
  - no network write could succeed at all: the image shipped only polkit's
    client libraries, not polkitd, so NetworkManager refused every
    connection-add-activate with "Not authorized to control networking."
    polkitd (with its setuid helper and unit) and a netdev rule now ship
  - the Wi-Fi panel is a wlr-layer-shell surface created with keyboard
    interactivity None and never changed, so the password field could not
    receive a keystroke; it now takes the keyboard (Exclusive) only while
    a password dialog is open and drops back when it closes
  - wl_keyboard.keymap is NUL-terminated and counts the terminator, so
    CString::new failed silently and every machine fell back to a built-in
    US layout whatever keyboard it had; the string is trimmed first
  - brightness writes ran synchronously on the event loop, always reported
    success, and preferred firmware acpi_video stubs; panic = "abort" turned
    any panic into the reported "desktop crashed and came back"
  - every pointer move during a volume drag spawned a new wpctl process and
    repainted every surface including the wallpaper, and the 30 s status
    poll ran nmcli/bluetoothctl/wpctl on the event loop

Verified in QEMU/OVMF from this ISO: boots to the desktop with no panic and
no keymap warning; 11 typed characters in the Wi-Fi password field produce
11 dots and NetworkManager stores exactly that PSK (wpa-psk/Testpass123);
pkcheck authorizes network-control for the desktop user and the audit
refusal is gone; a hotspot is listed as joinable rather than "Connected";
a volume click shows 155% and wpctl get-volume reports 1.55; refurb-control
volume/mute move the sink and return 0; a fake backlight row appears, writes,
hides itself on failure and the shell survives; refurb-control brightness
exits non-zero with a reason instead of claiming success; refurb-diag writes
its archive; 16/16 unit tests pass.

Not verified: a real radio associating with an access point end to end (the
emulated AP and client share one host and NM's scan tears the AP down), real
panel backlight writes, the media/brightness keys and Super combinations
themselves (QEMU does not deliver those keycodes), physical keyboard layouts,
and the brightness crash, which is addressed structurally. Details:
docs/hardware-0.6.md
